28.11.m:
And the Aṃśu cup mine, and the Raśmi cup mine;
and the Adābhya cup mine, and the Adhipati cup mine;
and the Upāṃśu cup mine, and the Antaryāma cup mine;
and the cup for Indra and Vāyu mine, and the cup for Mitra and Varuṇa mine;
and the cup for the Aśvins mine, and the Pratiprasthāna cup mine;
and the Śukra cup mine, and the Manthin cup mine;
and the Āgrayaṇa cup mine, and the lesser Vaiśvadeva cup mine;
and the Dhruva cup mine, and the Vaiśvānara cup mine;
and the cup for Indra and Agni mine, and the Vaiśvadeva cup mine;
and the Marutvatīya cup mine, and the Mahendrīya cup mine;
and the Sāvitra cup mine, and the Sārasvata cup mine;
and the Pātnīvata cup mine, and the Hāryojana cup mine;
and the offering ladles mine, and the cups mine;
and the Vāyavya vessels mine, and the wooden trough mine;
and the Pūtabhṛt vessel mine, and the Ādhavanīya vessel mine;
and the pressing stones mine, and the two pressing boards mine;
and the final bath mine, and the Svagākāra cry mine;
and the fire mine, and the Gharma vessel [mine];
and Arka mine, and Sūrya mine;
and the breath mine, and the horse sacrifice mine;
and the earth mine, and Aditi mine;
and Diti mine, and heaven mine;
and the Śakvarī meters, the fingers, and the quarters, let them be ordered for me through the sacrifice;
and the vow, and the season;
and the year, and ascetic heat;
and day and night, the two thighs, the two knees, and the Bṛhat and Rathantara [sāmans], let them be ordered for me through the sacrifice.

29.1.m:
And the eighteen-month-old bull mine, and the eighteen-month-old heifer mine;
and the two-year-old bull mine, and the two-year-old cow mine;
and the thirty-month-old bull mine, and the thirty-month-old cow mine;
and the three-year-old bull mine, and the three-year-old cow mine;
and the four-year-old bull mine, and the four-year-old cow mine;
and the five-year-old bull mine, and the five-year-old cow mine;
and the breeding bull mine, and the barren cow mine;
and the draft ox mine, and the milch cow mine;
and the bull mine, and the cow that miscarries mine;
and one mine, and three mine;
and three mine, and thirty-three mine;
and four mine, and eight mine;
and eight mine, and forty-eight mine.
And vigor and impulse,
and the after-born and resolve,
and the lord of speech and wealth,
heaven, head of the head,
belonging to Vyaśva, rich in horses,
penultimate and ultimate,
child of Bhuvana, lord of the world,
Prajāpati, the sovereign ruler.
This is your kingdom;
you are the restrainer, the ruler for Mitra.
For strength you;
for rain you;
for sovereignty over creatures you.
May life-span be ordered through the sacrifice;
may sacrifice be ordered through the sacrifice;
and ṛk and sāman,
and stoma and yajus,
and Bṛhat and Rathantara.
To heaven, O gods, have we gone; children of Prajāpati have we become; immortal have we become, vaṭ, svāhā!

29.8.m:
With [verses] to Savitṛ he takes the spade,
for instigation.
This [earth] is the gāyatrī, the atmosphere is the triṣṭubh, the sky is the jagatī, the directions are the anuṣṭubh;
with the meters he turns it toward himself from these worlds.
It is sharpened at both ends.
With a plowshare sharpened at one end so much food is produced;
food is the sun-plant, the sun-plant is Agni;
for the acquisition of food, of the sun-plant.
He should make it a cubit in length.
By the Puruṣa indeed is the sacrifice measured;
[he makes it] measured by the sacrifice-puruṣa.
He should make it a fathom in length;
such indeed is the power in man;
measured by power.
He should make it without fixed measure,
for the acquisition of the unmeasured.
He should make it of a tree that bears fruit;
this indeed is the most powerful among trees;
for possessiveness of power.
He should make it spotted, of bamboo, hollow.
Agni departed from the gods;
he entered bamboo;
he fastened these armor-plates that are the joints;
he wandered along this world which is hollow;
wherever he stayed, that he burned out;
that became spotted.
When it is spotted, of bamboo, hollow,
it is most connected with Agni;
for prosperity,
and also for the acquisition of whatever of him is hidden here.
Agni departed from the gods;
wherever he stayed, Prajāpati saw him following.
Prajāpati's is the horse;
when they go with a horse,
[it is] for the tracing of Agni.
By this indeed the gods overcame the asuras at the end;
when they go with a horse,
[it is] for the overcoming of the rival.
This the rakṣases did not cross over;
when they go with a horse,
[it is] for crossing beyond the rakṣases.
They lead the horse in front, the donkey behind,
for distinguishing the worse from the better;
therefore after the virtuous one going in front, the worse follows behind.
He gathers with a donkey;
therefore this one, among animals depositing semen, is the lowest of equal [beasts];
for Agni burned out his semen.
When he gathers with this one strength, the sun-plant into this [earth],
therefore this one is the most long-lived in it.
'Run forward swiftly, O prize-winner!'
'Do you two harness the donkey!'
This is for the horse and the donkey.
'In every harnessing we summon for aid the stronger Indra, in every prize-fight,
we companions [summon him] for help'—
for help, indeed, for the prize is Agni piled up.
'Come conquering, trampling the curses!'
Abundant indeed is the rival of one who prospers;
he prospers who piles up Agni;
the horse is a thunderbolt;
sin, curse, is the rival;
with the thunderbolt indeed he tramples on sin, the rival.
'In the overlordship of Rudra, come bringing bliss!'
Belonging to Rudra indeed are cattle;
having begged the cattle from Rudra, he performs the work for himself.
'Pervade the broad atmosphere!'
'Securing pasture, bringing freedom from danger, together with Pūṣan as companion'—
Pūṣan indeed is the guide of paths—
for attainment.
'From the seat of the earth [bring] Agni [with the earth-fill like the Aṅgirases]!'
[For Agni has the earth-fill as his abode;]
the Aṅgirases gathered him at the beginning with deities;
he gathers him with deities into his proper abode.
'Let us go to Agni with the earth-fill like the Aṅgirases,' he says to the one he meets;
every person indeed possesses fire;
he appropriates his prize.
Having announced to Prajāpati, Agni is to be piled up; having announced to the king;
for he is most belonging to Prajāpati.
This [earth] indeed is Prajāpati;
this anthill is its ear.
When, having dug up the anthill-lump, he addresses it,
having announced it to Prajāpati himself he piles up Agni.
They hear of him having piled up Agni:
'He has piled up Agni';
for ear tells to ear.

30.1.m:
"Agni has looked along the forepart of the dawns," [he recites],
for surveying.
"Having arrived, the courser shakes off all assailants on the way," [he recites];
with this [verse] he drives off assailants.
"Having stepped on the earth, O courser, seek Agni with your luster," [he recites].
Prajāpati sought him with the previous [verse]; he found [him] with the latter.
With the previous he seeks; with the latter he finds.
"Heaven is your back, earth your seat," [he recites].
The horse, belonging to Prajāpati, is armed with a thunderbolt.
[He surpasses] animals with teeth in both jaws by means of his hairs, and those with teeth in one jaw by his teeth.
With the thunderbolt indeed he treads down the rival.
Where a counterpart of the sacrifice is made, there matching cattle are born.
"Step forth!" [and] "He has stepped forth"—with two matching [verses] he makes [him] step forth.
Therefore matching cattle are born.
With two [verses];
the sacrificer has two feet,
for firm standing.
Whatever oblation the Adhvaryu offers where there is no fire, the Adhvaryu becomes blind,
and the rakṣases strike the sacrifice.
Agni lusted after the wives of Varuṇa.
His splendor fell away;
that became gold.
Having thrown down that gold, he offers;
he offers [as it were] into a fire,
for success.
The Adhvaryu does not become blind;
the rakṣases do not strike the sacrifice.
"I sprinkle Agni with mind, with ghee," [he recites].
Mind indeed is swifter than speech;
with the mind indeed he reaches the offering.
"Who faces all creatures," [he recites];
therefore this one dwells facing all creatures.
"The widest, violent, wise food," [he recites];
food indeed he sweetens for him.
"I sprinkle you with speech, with ghee," [he recites];
he repeats what was just spoken before.
"With an uninjurious mind do you enjoy that," [he recites],
for the warding off of rakṣases.
"Agni, fair as a youth, of desirable color, rejoiced with his body not to be touched," [he recites];
respect indeed he establishes in him.
He offers with two [verses];
the sacrificer has two feet,
for firm standing.
At every face of the sacrifice the rakṣases wish to strike the sacrifice.
In that he draws a line around,
[it is] for the warding off of the rakṣases.
He draws a line around with three [verses];
three are these worlds;
from these worlds indeed he drives away the rakṣases.
"Around the lord of vigor, the sage," he draws a line around with a gāyatrī;
the gāyatrī is the brahman;
with the brahman he encloses it.
"Around you, O Agni, we as a rampart," with an anuṣṭubh;
speech is the anuṣṭubh;
the anuṣṭubh encompasses all the meters;
with speech indeed he encloses all the meters.
"You, O Agni, with days," with a triṣṭubh;
vigor is the triṣṭubh;
with vigor indeed he encloses it.
With an anuṣṭubh he draws a line in the middle;
speech is the anuṣṭubh;
speech indeed he places in the middle;
therefore speech speaks from the middle.
Splendor is the gāyatrī;
the sacrifice is the anuṣṭubh;
sensory power is the triṣṭubh;
with splendor and sensory power he encloses the sacrifice on both sides.

30.2.m:
With two [verses] to Savitṛ he digs,
for instigation.
Smoke indeed he finds with the former, light with the latter.
With two [verses];
the sacrificer has two feet,
for firm standing.
"You are the back of the waters, the womb of Agni," [with this] he takes a lotus leaf;
for this is the back of the waters, the womb of Agni.
In that he collects with a lotus leaf,
he collects it with its own womb,
for appeasement, that it may not burn down.
He collects with a black antelope skin;
the black antelope skin is the sacrifice;
with the sacrifice indeed he collects the sacrifice,
so that there is no spilling;
for the sacrifice does not spill in the sacrifice.
This indeed is the form of the brahman, namely the black antelope skin;
with the brahman and with the ṛc and sāman he collects it.
"You two are protection and armor," [saying this] he spreads them out.
This [earth] is the lotus leaf, yonder [heaven] is the black antelope skin;
these two indeed he spreads out.
"Both unperforated and thick," [he recites];
for both these two are unperforated and thick.
"Spacious," [he recites];
therefore these two are spacious.
"Bear the Agni belonging to the loose earth," [he recites];
for he belongs to the loose earth.
"Dwell together, finding the sun, united by heart through yourself," [he recites];
therefore these two, being distinct, are united.
"About to bear within Agni, radiant, unceasing," [he recites];
he places unceasing light in him.
"You belong to the loose earth, you bear all," [he recites];
for this one belongs to the loose earth, bears all.
"Atharvan first churned you out, O Agni," [he recites];
Prajāpati is Atharvan;
Prajāpati churned this one in the beginning;
Prajāpati indeed generates him.
"And you Dadhyañc the seer," [he recites];
Dadhyañc Ātharvaṇa was full of splendor;
with him he collects him.
"And you Pathya the bull," [he recites];
he repeats what was just spoken before.
With four [verses] he heaps [earth] together;
four indeed are the meters;
with the meters indeed he heaps it together.
For a brāhmaṇa he should heap together with gāyatrī verses;
for the brāhmaṇa belongs to the gāyatrī.
With triṣṭubh verses for a rājanya;
for the rājanya belongs to the triṣṭubh.
With jagatī verses for a vaiśya;
for the vaiśya belongs to the jagatī.
He should heap together with both according to the meter for one whom he desires, "May he be wealthier."
Splendor is the gāyatrī;
sensory power is the triṣṭubh;
splendor and sensory power he places concordantly in him.
With eight [verses] he heaps together;
eight-syllabled is the gāyatrī;
Agni belongs to the gāyatrī;
as great as Agni is, so much he collects.
"Sit down, O Hotṛ," [he recites];
deities indeed he settles in him.
"Down, O Hotṛ," [he recites];
men indeed.
"Sit down together," [he recites];
birds indeed.
"For the noble one was born at the beginning of days," [he recites];
gods and men who are settled he generates in him.

30.3.m:
"Send forth the divine waters containing sweetness," [he recites],
for the firm standing of the plants.
"From their standing-place let the plants arise bearing good berries," [he recites];
therefore the plants seize fruit.
"May Vāyu Mātariśvan unite [it] for you," [he recites];
therefore Vāyu carries the rain.
Vāyu is breath;
he places breath in it.
"For that, O god, let there be vaṣaṭ to you," [he recites].
There are six seasons;
he places the rain in the seasons;
therefore it rains season by season.
If he were to utter vaṣaṭ visibly, the vaṣaṭ-call would become stale;
if he were not to utter vaṣaṭ, the rakṣases would strike the sacrifice.
"For that, O god, let there be vaṣaṭ to you," is cryptically [stated];
the vaṣaṭ-call does not become stale, and the rakṣases do not strike the sacrifice.
If he were to offer with clarified butter, he would afflict the earth with heat;
he pours down water,
for appeasement, that it may not burn down.
"Well-born, with light he has reached the refuge, the defense, the heaven;
put on the garment of all forms, O Agni, shining with light," [he recites].
“Thus,” [it is said].
The meters are Agni's garment;
with the meters he clothes him.
With an anuṣṭubh;
the anuṣṭubh is Agni's dear body;
with his dear body he clothes him.
One who knows thus becomes a finder of garments.
He who is bound up is Varuṇa's wrath.
"Stand up, O you of good sacrifice; upright for our aid," with these [verses] they impel Varuṇa's wrath upward.
With two [verses] he brings it down;
the sacrificer has two feet,
for firm standing.
"Born, you are the embryo of the two worlds, O Agni, lovely, distributed among the plants," [he recites];
therefore Agni is along all the plants.
"Neighing aloud, go forth from the mothers," [he recites].
The plants are his mothers;
from them he leads him along.
"Be firm, strong-limbed," [he recites];
he places firmness in the donkey;
therefore it is the best load-bearer among animals.
"Be kind to human offspring, you, O Aṅgiras," [he recites].
He causes pain to creatures now,
for appeasement.
"Do not burn heaven and earth, nor the atmosphere, nor the trees," [he recites];
he appeases him from these worlds.
"Let the courser go forth, neighing, braying loudly, let the donkey fly," [he recites],
for attainment.
"Carrying the Agni of the earth, may he not fall before his life-span," [he recites];
he places life in it;
therefore the donkey reaches a full life-span;
therefore when a donkey dies, they are afraid.
"The bull carrying the bull-Agni," [he recites];
for as a bull, this one carries the bull.
"The embryo of the waters, oceanic," [he recites];
for he is the embryo of the waters.
"O Agni, come for enjoyment," [he recites].
By Agni the gods formerly partook of this;
for enjoyment.
Having moved from his resting-place, he has not yet reached a firm standing;
he thinks of the sacrificer and the Adhvaryu.
"Cosmic order, truth; cosmic order, truth," [he recites].
This [earth] is cosmic order; yonder [sky] is truth;
in these two he establishes him.
"O plants, receive this Agni coming kindly towards you here," [he recites].
The plants are his share;
he makes him united with them;
moreover, from which [plants] he moves him, in those he establishes him.
"Flowering, bearing good berries," [he recites];
therefore the plants seize fruit.
With two [verses] he brings it down;
the sacrificer has two feet,
for firm standing.
"With your strength," he loosens [it];
he unties Varuṇa's wrath.
"Waters, you are indeed bringing bliss," with this he sends forth the waters.
The waters are peaceful;
with peaceful [waters] he appeases his heat.
With three [verses];
threefold is Agni;
as great as Agni is, so great a heat of his he appeases.
He mixes [it] with goat's hair.
The goat is Agni's dear body;
with his dear body he mixes him.
He mixes [it] with gravel,
for stability.
He mixes [it] with potsherds from a ruin.
He afflicts the wild animals with heat;
therefore, though being born equal to [other] animals, these are the smallest;
for they are afflicted with heat.

30.4.m:
"Mitra, having mixed the earth..." [he recites].
This indeed is Varuṇa's wrath;
with Mitra indeed he approaches Varuṇa's wrath.
"The Rudras, having mixed the earth... Mixed by the Vasus, by the Rudras..." [he recites].
With these deities Prajāpati mixed it;
with them indeed he mixes it.
With some [mantras] he holds forth;
with others he recites formulas over [it];
he makes a pairing.
Moreover, right from the middle he secures the blessing of the sacrifice.
"You are the head of Makha," [he recites];
Makha is the sacrifice;
he makes the head of the sacrifice.
"May the Vasus make you with the Gāyatrī meter, like Aṅgiras," [he recites].
With these deities Prajāpati made it;
with them indeed he makes it.
He makes it three-tiered;
these worlds are three;
he attains these very worlds.
"Hold offspring in me, increase of wealth, overlordship of cows," [he recites];
this is a blessing, the milking of the meters.
He should make it a cubit in size.
The sacrifice is measured by man,
measured in size with the sacrifice-man.
As much as he can encompass with both arms, that big he should make it;
this much indeed is the virility in a man;
measured in size with virility.
He should make it a span in size;
this much is the mouth;
Agni is the mouth of the gods;
measured in size with the mouth.
He should make it unmeasured,
for the attainment of the unmeasured.
He should make it four-nippled,
for the milking of Aditi;
eight-nippled,
for the milking of the Gāyatrī;
he should make it six-nippled,
for the milking of the seasons;
he should make it two-nippled,
for the milking of heaven and earth.
For sorcery he should make it nine-cornered;
the thunderbolt is threefold;
he hurls a thunderbolt against the rival.
"You are the girdle of Aditi; may Aditi grasp your rim," with this Yajus verse he makes the rim.
For men make [it] without a Yajus verse;
he differentiates it from a human vessel.
If it were to break before baking, the sacrificer would suffer calamity, his sacrifice would be destroyed.
"Having made the great pan, earthen womb for Agni..." [he recites];
he hands it over to the deities
for protection.
"May the Vasus fumigate you with the Gāyatrī meter, like Aṅgiras," [he recites].
With these deities Prajāpati fumigated it;
with them indeed he fumigates it.
He fumigates it with horse-dung;
the horse belongs to Prajāpati;
Prajāpati is the sacrifice;
for possessing the same womb.
He fumigates with seven [verses];
the pan is the head of the sacrifice;
the breaths are seven;
he places the breaths in the head itself;
therefore the breaths in the head are seven.

30.5.m:
"May the divine Aditi, accompanied by the Viśvedevas..." [he recites];
Aditi is this [earth];
with Aditi he digs into Aditi,
so as not to make a wound in her;
for one's own does not injure one's own.
"May the divine wives of the gods, accompanied by the Viśvedevas..." [he recites].
The wives of the gods placed this [pan] here in the beginning;
with them indeed he places it.
"May the divine Dhiṣaṇās, accompanied by the Viśvedevas..." [he recites];
the Dhiṣaṇās are knowledge;
with knowledge indeed he enkindles it.
"May the divine Varūtrīs, accompanied by the Viśvedevas..." [he recites];
the Varūtrīs are the Hotrās;
with the Hotrās indeed he boils it.
"May the divine Gnās, accompanied by the Viśvedevas..." [he recites];
the Gnās are the meters;
with the meters indeed he bakes it.
"May the divine ladies with unbroken wings, accompanied by the Viśvedevas..." [he recites];
the ladies are the wives of the gods;
with them indeed he bakes it.
"Let them bake twice," he says;
therefore the crop ripens twice a year.
He bakes with six [verses];
the seasons are six;
with the seasons the wives of the gods baked it;
with them indeed he bakes it.
Enkindled, this [pan] is Varuṇa's wrath;
"Of Mitra, supporter of men..." [he recites];
with Mitra indeed he approaches Varuṇa's wrath.
"May the divine Savitṛ take you out," with a verse to Savitṛ he takes [it] out,
for instigation.
"Without trembling, fill the regions, the quarters on earth," [he recites];
therefore Agni shines through all quarters.
"Arise, become great, stand upright, be firm," [he recites];
he strengthens it.
If it were to break, the sacrificer would suffer calamity, his sacrifice would be destroyed.
"O Mitra, I entrust this pan to you," [he recites];
Mitra is the Brahman;
he entrusts it to the Brahman.
If it should break, having mixed [clay] with those potsherds, he should make another;
that alone is then the atonement for the sacrifice.
"May the Vasus moisten you with the Gāyatrī meter, like Aṅgiras," [he recites].
With meters it is made, with meters it is fumigated, with meters it is baked;
with meters he moistens it, with its own abode.
Demonic is a vessel that is not moistened;
when he moistens it,
he places it among the gods.
He moistens it with goat's milk;
for that is most related to Agni,
and also with the highest milk.

30.6.m:
Now these victim animals are seized for the Agnis [that are] desires;
the desires are the Agnis;
with these he wins all desires, he attains all desires.
If he were not to seize the animals, his animals would remain unobtained;
if he were to complete [the sacrifice with them], their heads would become stale;
if he were not to complete [the rite], he would sever the sacrifice.
In that he seizes the animals,
by that indeed he obtains the animals.
In that he releases them after they have been carried around with fire,
[it is for the non-staleness of the heads ...]

30.7.m:
[When he] pours out [an oblation to Agni Vaiśvānara],
he obtains that desire for the sake of which desire it is given.
In that it is on twelve potsherds,
the year has twelve months;
the creatures that fall, fall from the year;
those that stand firm, stand firm in the year;
having stood firm in the year, he bears Agni.

30.8.m:
He offers six adhīta formulas;
there are six seasons;
with the seasons indeed he consecrates him, with the seasons he raises up his vigor.
He offers these seven;
there are seven breaths;
with the breaths indeed he consecrates him.
He offers separately;
therefore the breaths have different powers: eye, ear, and speech.
With an anuṣṭubh verse he offers the last;
speech is the anuṣṭubh;
he places speech as the highest;
therefore speech, the highest of the breaths, speaks [what is] arranged.
The anuṣṭubh is all meters;
cattle are meters;
food is cattle;
he obtains cattle, [which are] food.
In that he puts [the pan] on the flames, by that he obtains the past;
in that [he puts it] on the glowing coals, by that [he obtains] the future.
It is to be put on the glowing coals;
for the future is greater than the past.
"Do not break, do not be harmed," [he says];
he makes it firm.
"Unharmed, rise up in this sacrifice," [he says],
for attainment.
With two [verses] he puts it on;
two-footed is the sacrificer;
[it is] for firm standing.
For one wishing to thrive, [the fire should be produced] by encircling friction.
The consecrated one is an embryo;
he comes to be from here, as it were;
from here indeed he brings him forth.
Having churned [fire], he should deposit it for one who has attained prosperity;
for he has come into being;
he approaches his own deity.
Having brought it from elsewhere, he should deposit it for him of whom he might desire, "May I produce a rival for him";
he produces a rival for him.
Having brought it from a frying pan, he should deposit it for one desiring food;
food is prepared in a frying pan;
he obtains food together with its source.
For one desiring heaven, he should deposit [fire from] whatever tree blazes above;
this among fires is heavenly;
[it is] for the attainment of the heavenly world.
"Whose food is wood, whose libation is clarified butter," [saying this,] he should deposit a furrowed [stick of] betel nut tree, having anointed it with clarified butter.
With that which was Agni's dear body, he entered into the betel nut tree;
clarified butter is splendor;
he anoints his dear body with splendor.
He deposits reed stalks;
reed stalks are vigor;
he covers him with vigor.
"From the highest realm," [saying this, he deposits a kindling stick] of udumbara wood;
where the gods divided vigor, there the udumbara tree was born;
he covers him, as soon as born, with vigor.
"From the furthest distance," [saying this, one] of vikankata wood;
when Agni was created, his radiance reached the vikankata tree;
he obtains that very [radiance].
"Whatever [sticks of wood], O Agni," "what the small ant eats" [he recites];
formerly [fire] would not burn what was felled by an axe for fire;
Prayoga the seer sweetened it for him with these [verses];
in that he places the kindling stick with these [verses],
he sweetens firewood for him;
his food becomes sweetened.
The meters are Agni's womb;
in that he places it with meters,
he bears him along in his own womb.
"Night after night, bringing [it] without slackening," [he recites];
he prays for a blessing.
"Agni being kindled at the navel of the earth," [he recites];
he conquers armies with this [verse].
"The armies that are advancing," [he recites];
when Agni was born, the demons sought to slay him;
the gods saw these verses;
with them they smote away the demons from him;
in that he places the kindling sticks with these [verses],
[it is] for striking away the demons.
The gods and the asuras contended;
the gods saw this Mālimlava [hymn];
with it they overcame the asuras;
in that he places a kindling stick with this [verse],
[it is] for overcoming the rival.
Therefore, in whatever year they pile the fire altar, they are apt to seize the thief.
"The armies that are advancing," [saying this, he places a kindling stick] of udumbara wood;
he covers him with vigor.
"With two tusks the robbers, O Agni," [saying this, one] of aśvattha wood;
this among trees overcomes rivals;
[it is] for victory.
When he says, "With two tusks," he should meditate in his mind on him whom he hates;
the mind is swifter than speech;
he offers him as an oblation to Agni.
"The robbers who are among people," [saying this, one] of vikankata wood;
when Agni was created, his radiance reached the vikankata tree;
he obtains that very [radiance].
"He who would act as an enemy toward us," [saying this, one] of śamī wood;
[it is] for appeasement.
"He who would blame us and would wish to harm [us]," [he recites];
therefore one should not speak ill of one who has piled the fire altar.
"Sharpened is my prayer," [he recites];
with prayer he sharpens power, with power prayer;
therefore power having prayer as domestic priest excels other powers;
therefore prayer endowed with power excels other brahmins.
"I have raised up their arms, [their] brilliance, and their strength," [he recites];
he prays for a blessing.
"I destroy the enemies with prayer, I raise up my own," [he recites],
according to the formula.

31.1.m:
With [the verse], 'Beholding, the golden ornament has shone widely,' he fastens on the golden ornament.
Agni is death;
gold is immortality;
with immortality indeed he separates [himself] from death.
It has twenty-one knobs
for standing firm;
the worlds of the gods are twenty-one;
from them indeed he drives out his rival.
He should make the knobs on the outside;
from this world indeed he drives him out.
Fastening it on, he throws it around on both sides;
from both worlds indeed he excludes him.
With [the words], 'Night and dawn,'
with day and night indeed he surrounds him.
With [the words], 'The gods, giving wealth, held Agni,'
the gods, giving wealth, are the breaths;
with the breaths indeed he has held him.
The sling has six suspensions;
there are six seasons;
with the seasons indeed he surrounds him.
It has twelve suspensions;
twelve months are the year;
with the year indeed he surrounds him.
It is made of muñja grass;
muñja grass is vigor;
with vigor indeed he surrounds him.
He should not bear it below the navel;
Agni would burn up his semen.
He should bear it above the navel;
what is above the navel belongs to the gods;
Agni is all the deities;
he bears the deities where what belongs to the gods is.
With [the verse], 'The wise [god] assumes all forms,'
he fastens it on with a verse to Savitṛ,
for impulse.
Sitting, he fastens it on;
therefore sitting offspring are born.
With [the words], 'You are the eagle, winged,'
these are the materials for Agni;
with these indeed he collects Agni;
this is Agni's dear domain;
that indeed he obtains.
With [the words], 'Go to heaven, fly to the light,'
[it is] for the attainment of the heavenly world.
These worlds belonged to the Asuras;
those gods said to Viṣṇu: 'Give us as much as this youth strides across.'
Once he strode across this [earth], the gāyatrī meter; once across the atmosphere, the triṣṭubh meter; once across heaven, the jagatī meter; once across the quarters, the anuṣṭubh meter.
Those gods stripped these worlds from the Asuras;
then the gods flourished, the Asuras were defeated.
He who, knowing thus, steps the strides, strips these very worlds from his rival;
he flourishes himself, his rival is defeated.
With [the verse], 'Agni cried out like thundering heaven,'
by this indeed Vatsaprī Bhālandana obtained Agni's dear domain;
by this indeed he obtains Agni's dear domain.
With 'O Agni, returning,' 'O Agni, Aṅgiras,' 'Again with vigor,' 'Together with wealth,' he returns;
therefore domestic animals come in the evening from the forest to the village.
With four [verses];
for animals are four-footed.
He turns around to the right;
therefore the right half of oneself has greater vigor.
With [the words], 'I have brought you,'
for he brings him;
with [the words], 'May all the clans desire you, establish the kingdom in us,'
over whomever he might desire that there should be a kingdom, him he should mentally contemplate;
it becomes indeed a kingdom.
With [the verse], 'Untie the highest fetter from us, O Varuṇa,'
Śunaḥśepa Ājīgarti, seized by Varuṇa, saw this [verse];
by it indeed he was freed from Varuṇa's fetter;
by it indeed he releases Varuṇa's fetter.
With [the verse], 'High, great before the dawns, he has stood up,'
darkness is evil;
by it indeed he strikes away evil.
With [the verse], 'The swan sitting in purity,'
by it indeed he gratifies the seven [vital breaths].
With [the word], 'Existing,'
he establishes him in what exists in regular succession.
He places [the pan] on a chair;
he of the Agnis who is borne in the pan is indeed consecrated [as king];
therefore he places [it] on a chair.
If he were to place [it] below, the embryos would fall out;
he places [it] above,
for the holding of the embryos, so they do not fall out.
He attends [the fire] with three [verses];
the breaths are three: prāṇa, vyāna, and apāna;
those indeed he places in the sacrificer.
These worlds are three;
in these worlds indeed he thrives.

31.2.m:
Now this Vātsapra [hymn]:
by this, indeed, Vatsaprī Bhālandana obtained the dear abode of Agni.
Through this, one obtains the dear abode of Agni.
By this, indeed, he overcame the superior talk of the ṛṣis.
Through this, one overcomes evil, superior talk.
By this, indeed, he conquered curses.
Curses indeed conquers whoever, knowing thus, worships with this.
In that he strides strides,
thereby he maintains the march;
in that he worships,
thereby [he maintains] peace.
On the previous day he strides, on the following day he worships;
therefore the mind of some creatures is on the march, [the mind] of others on peace.
Therefore the wanderer is lord of peace;
therefore the wanderer settles upon what belongs to peace.
It consists of eleven [verses];
the triṣṭubh has eleven syllables;
[the triṣṭubh is vigor;]
he obtains vigor.
In old times, indeed, a cart had a single pole.
Then the ṛṣis saw this two-poled setting-up for Agni;
therefore they make a two-poled setting-up for Agni.
In imitation of that, this two-poled cart is made.
With [the words], 'Let all the gods [raise] you up,'
all the gods are breaths;
with the breaths indeed he raises him up.
All the gods are men;
with men indeed he raises him up.
With [the words], 'O Agni, let them bring [you] with thoughts,'
for whatever purpose he raises him up, with that he makes him prosper.
With [the words], 'Go forward, radiant Agni,'
at that time, indeed, he burns against creatures;
[this is] for appeasement.
With [the words], 'Agni has roared,' if it should creak;
for with roaring indeed they convey with a cart.
Therefore one who possesses a cart and one who possesses a chariot are most honored of guests.
With [the words], 'O lord of food, give us of the food,'
food indeed he makes sweet to him.
With [the words], 'Of [the food] that is free from disease and vigorous,'
'free from consumption' is what he says.
With [the words], 'Advance the giver, bestow vigor upon us, for two-footed and four-footed,'
he utters a blessing.
With [the words], 'Honor Agni with fuel,' having anointed [it] with ghee, he lays on a fuel-stick;
just as he cooks a meal with clarified butter for a brahmin guest, just so is that.
With a gāyatrī [verse] he should lay [it] on for a brahmin;
for a brahmin belongs to the gāyatrī;
with a triṣṭubh for a noble;
for a noble belongs to the triṣṭubh;
with a jagatī for a vaiśya;
for a vaiśya belongs to the jagatī;
according to the meter alone.
With [the words], 'Divine Waters, receive this ash,' he scatters the ash into the water.
The waters are the womb of Agni;
he causes him to go to his own womb.
He scatters with three [verses];
threefold is Agni;
as great as Agni is, him he causes to go to a firm standing.
He casts Agni away who scatters ash into the water.
'Having settled into the womb, the water and the earth, with the ash, O Agni, / having united with the mothers, radiant, may you sit again,' / saying thus,
he places light in him.
He is deprived of vigor and cattle who scatters ash into the water.
With [the words], 'Again with vigor, with wealth,'
he obtains vigor and cattle again.
With [the words], 'Pay heed to this my speech, youngest one,'
therefore creatures, having slept, awaken.
With [the words], 'Be a patron, bounteous, bestower of wealth, lord of wealth,'
therefore cattle, having set forth and wandered, having returned, sit down each in its place.
With two [verses];
the sacrificer is two-footed;
for standing firm.

31.3.m:
With [the words], 'Go away, disperse, crawl apart,' he settles upon the place of sacrifice.
Whoever piles the fire without begging the place of sacrifice from its lord, for Yama his fires are piled.
Yama is its lord.
Begging the place of sacrifice from Yama, its lord, he piles the fire for himself.
Seeking even with the tip of an arrow an untouched [spot] of this [earth], they did not find it.
In that he settles upon the place of sacrifice with this [verse],
he piles the fire on an untouched [spot] of this [earth].
Sanācchava said: 'How many a formula has removed me from my abode?'
Agni is to be piled in the dear abode of heaven.
Saline soil is the dear abode of heaven.
In that he scatters saline soil,
he piles Agni in the dear abode of heaven.
The sands are indeed these unlimited Vaiśvānara bricks.
In that he strews sands,
he secures them.
[With the verse], 'This is that Agni in whom Indra [drank] soma,'
this is the hymn of Viśvāmitra.
By this indeed Viśvāmitra secured the dear abode of Agni;
by this he secures the dear abode of Agni.
He places four facing east.
Four indeed are the meters;
by the meters the gods went to the heavenly world.
Their quarters collapsed together;
they saw these quarter-bricks;
with them they strengthened the quarters.
In that he places two facing together in front and two facing together behind,
[it is] for holding apart the quarters.
He places eight of these;
eight syllables has the gāyatrī;
the gāyatrī knows straight to the heavenly world,
for knowing the heavenly world.
He places eight of these;
eight syllables has the gāyatrī;
Agni is of the nature of the gāyatrī;
as great as Agni is, so much he piles him.
Having placed these eight, he places thirteen space-fillers;
they amount to twenty-one.
The twenty-first is the support of the stomas;
the gārhapatya is a support;
following the support of the twenty-first, he stands firm with the gārhapatya.
He who knows thus stands facing the piled fire.
One who piles for the first time should pile it five-layered;
fivefold is the sacrifice;
fivefold are cattle;
he secures the sacrifice and cattle.
One who piles for the second time should pile it three-layered;
three are these worlds;
in these worlds he thrives.
One who piles for the last time should pile it single-layered;
singly indeed he goes to the heavenly world.
There are five layers, five [scatterings of] loose earth;
that makes ten;
ten syllables has the virāj; the virāj is food;
in the virāj, in food, he stands firm.
The bricks are bone, the loose earth is flesh.
In that he covers it with loose earth,
therefore bone is covered with flesh.
He does not get a bad skin, who knows thus.
With [the words], 'Agreeing, may you two resolve together,' he pours [them] together.
Dominion comes together;
the formula is holy power;
with holy power indeed he pours the two together.
He pours together with four [formulas];
four indeed are the meters;
with the meters indeed he pours the two together.
In that having poured together he distributes,
therefore through holy power dominion unites, through holy power it separates.
'Like a mother her son, the pan has borne in her womb the earthly Agni, belonging to the loose earth;
her may all the gods in agreement with the seasons,'
having consecrated him with the seasons, he releases him with the seasons.
With [the words], 'May Prajāpati Viśvakarman release [thee],'
he makes Prajāpati his releaser.

31.4.m:
Now these three bricks to Nirṛti, black, baked with chaff.
In that they are black,
for this color attends him whom Nirṛti seizes.
In that they are baked with chaff,
this indeed is Nirṛti's share,
and with [her] share he satisfies [her].
With [the verse], 'What beyond this space,' with [a verse] to Vaiśvānara he takes [them];
he sweetens them.
They carry [them] to this quarter;
they carry to this quarter him whom Nirṛti seizes;
this indeed is Nirṛti's quarter;
in her own quarter he satisfies Nirṛti.
He places [them] in a naturally formed barren spot;
this indeed of this [earth] is seized by Nirṛti;
wherever of this [earth] is seized by Nirṛti, [there] he satisfies Nirṛti.
He places them facing away;
he satisfies Nirṛti [so that she turns] away.
He places three;
threefold arranged indeed is man;
from as much as his self is, he satisfies Nirṛti.
With [the words], 'Which bond goddess Nirṛti has bound upon thee,' he casts down the sling;
the fetter belongs to Nirṛti;
from Nirṛti's fetter he releases him.
To the world of the fathers they go, who proceed to the south with the bricks to Nirṛti;
the āhavanīya is the world of the gods;
in that he approaches the āhavanīya,
he returns to the world of the gods.
He approaches with a triṣṭubh;
vigor is the triṣṭubh;
vigor indeed he places in him;
with a single [verse] he places vigor in him in a single manner.

31.6.m:
He throws clods together from the quarters;
from the quarters he collects strength.
In strength indeed he piles the fire.
Whatever folk he might hate, from its quarter he should fetch [them], [saying]:
'Food and strength I have taken from here.'
Food indeed and strength he takes from it; it becomes hungry.
He pours out the high altar;
for on the high altar the fire is piled.
Moreover, he does not skip a sacrificial limb.
The high altar, indeed, is animals;
he obtains animals.
With a six-verse hymn, 'Agni, your fame, food,' he pours [it] out.
Six indeed are the seasons, the year;
the year is Agni Vaiśvānara.
By the form itself he obtains Vaiśvānara.
The first [verse] has eight syllables;
animals have eight hoofs;
he obtains animals.
Uneven and even are copulative,
for propagation.
'Ocean,' indeed, is the name of this metre of Prajāpati.
From the ocean animals are born,
for the propagation of animals.
Indra hurled the vajra at Vṛtra;
it became threefold: the wooden sword a third, the post a third, the chariot a third.
What shattered, those were the pebbles.
Agni is an animal;
the pebbles are the vajra.
In that he encloses with pebbles,
with the vajra indeed he surrounds the animals for him.
Therefore animals are held fast by the vajra.
Therefore the stronger does not take [from] the weaker.
He should enclose by tens for one who desires food;
ten-syllabled is the virāj;
the virāj is food;
he is established in the virāj, in food.
By nines for one who practices sorcery;
threefold is the vajra;
he hurls the vajra at his rival.
By sevens for one who desires animals;
seven indeed are the breaths;
from the breaths animals are born,
for the propagation of animals.
Without measuring the pebbles, he should disperse the sand [for him] whom he might wish: 'May he be without animals.'
Uncontained indeed he pours out his semen; he becomes without animals.
Having measured the pebbles, he should disperse the sand [for him] whom he might wish: 'May he have animals.'
Contained indeed he pours semen for him; he becomes possessed of animals.
The metres indeed are the womb of Agni; Soma is the placer of semen.
In that, having poured out with the metres, he disperses with [verses] to Soma,
in the womb indeed he places semen.
He disperses;
therefore embryos grow in the womb.
They have different beginnings [and] identical conclusions;
therefore from the same womb different forms are born.
With the gāyatrī he should disperse for a brāhmaṇa;
for the brāhmaṇa is of gāyatrī nature;
with the triṣṭubh for a rājanya;
for the rājanya is of triṣṭubh nature;
with the jagatī for a vaiśya;
for the vaiśya is of jagatī nature;
according to each metre indeed.

31.10.m:
Having poured down twenty-one beans, he brings the human head.
Beans are unholy, unholy is the human head;
with the unholy he buys the unholy.
There are twenty-one [beans];
twenty-onefold is man;
as great as his self is, so much he buys.
He places in exchange an anthill's core, perforated sevenfold.
This is indeed deprived of vital breaths;
seven are the vital breaths in the head;
he makes it prosper with the vital breaths.
He sings verses to Yama;
he snatches it from Yama's world.
He sings three [verses];
three are these worlds;
he snatches it from these worlds.
Therefore, nothing should be given to one who sings [them];
for the verse snatches it away.
Vital power departs from him: breath, head, vigor, sight, hearing, speech.
What is deprived of vital power is unholy;
they indeed call this a dead head.
Gold is holy;
in that he throws it [there] with flakes of gold,
he makes it holy, fit for sacrifice.
Having covered it in the pan, he throws it [there];
having caused it to reach a firm foundation, he makes it prosper with the vital breaths.
With 'You are a thousand-giver, for a thousand [I lay] you,' he lays down the human head.
Man indeed grants a thousand cattle;
the other cattle are a thousand.
In that he lays down the human head in the middle,
[it is] for unification.
Now these animal heads:
These thousandfold bricks Somadakṣa Kauśreya laid down for Śyāparṇa;
thence indeed he attained the thousandfold prosperity of cattle.
He who, knowing thus, lays these down, attains the thousandfold prosperity of cattle.
In the east he lays down the horse's head facing westward; in the west, the bull's [head] facing eastward.
He thus places cattle and horses facing one another in this [altar].
He should lay them down facing one another [for one] whom he would wish, 'May he have cattle';
he places the cattle facing one another in this [altar].
He should lay them down facing apart [for one] whom he would wish, 'May he be without cattle';
he makes the cattle turn their faces away from him.
The Adhvaryu should not pass between the animal heads;
this Agni is indeed called Yaviṣṭha ('the youngest');
he would separate his vital breaths, he would die.
Having laid down one, he attends upon [them] with all these [formulas];
he should set it down passing all the way around;
thereby all are laid down.
The Adhvaryu incurs no suffering, he does not fall into failure.
Just so many are cattle: two-footed and four-footed;
these he now causes to encounter distress.
With 'That wild one I point out for you, let distress reach him,'
he releases distress after the wild animals from the domestic animals.
Therefore, though being born equal to the cattle, these are the lowest;
for they have been smitten with distress.

31.11.m:
Agni is indeed an animal;
this womb of the animal is being prepared.
The water-bricks are seed;
in that the water-bricks are laid down,
he places seed in the womb itself.
When the animal turns into a mass, then it is born.
The meter-bricks are cattle;
in that he lays down the meter-bricks after the water-bricks,
[it is] for the propagation of cattle.
He lays down five of these;
fivefold are cattle;
as much as the animal is, so much he generates.
This [earth] indeed feared excessive burning by Agni;
she created these water-bricks;
she laid them down.
In that the water-bricks are laid down,
[it is] for non-burning of this [earth].
This [earth] indeed said: 'He shall eat food through the priesthood, for whom these are laid down.'
He who, knowing thus, lays these down, eats food through the priesthood.
He lays down five in the east, facing westward;
therefore an animal is born in the east, facing westward.
Five in the south, facing northward;
therefore in the south a male lies beside a female.
Five in the west, facing eastward;
therefore in the west seed is placed facing eastward.
Five in the north are the meter-bricks;
the meter-bricks are cattle;
the cattle have their abode in the north;
he sweeps around the cattle that have been born toward their own abode,
for the non-injury of the cattle.
He should not lay down water-bricks in the north;
if he were to lay them down, Varuṇa would seize the offspring all around.
After the water-bricks he lays down the breath-bearing bricks;
when seed has been poured, he places breath, mind, sight, hearing, and speech in it;
therefore an animal is born breathing, seeing, hearing, and speaking.
Those that possess breath, with 'This in front is the earth,' he lays down in the east;
he places breath in front;
therefore an animal breathes forward.
Those that possess mind, with 'This on the right is Viśvakarman,' in the south;
he places mind in the south;
therefore the right half is more endowed with mind;
therefore they approach the mind from the south.
Those that possess sight, with 'This behind is the all-encompassing,' in the west;
he places sight in the west;
therefore it sees forward.
Those that possess hearing, with 'This on the left is heaven,' in the north;
he places hearing in the north;
therefore an animal hears more from the north.
Those that possess speech, with 'This above is intellect,' in the middle;
he places speech in the middle;
therefore speech speaks from the middle.
He lays them down crosswise;
therefore cattle go striking their feet crosswise.
With those in the east, Vasiṣṭha prospered;
with those in the south, Bharadvāja;
with those in the west, Jamadagni;
with those in the north, Viśvāmitra;
with those in the middle, Viśvakarman.
He who thus knows their prosperity prospers;
he who thus knows their arrangement, for him it is arranged;
he who thus knows their relationship, becomes possessed of kin;
he who thus knows their primary cause, becomes possessed of a foundation;
he who thus knows their abode, becomes possessed of an abode;
he who thus knows their firm foundation, reaches a firm foundation.

31.12.m:
Now these bricks to the Aśvins [are laid down].
Agni is indeed a collapsed sacrifice;
who indeed knows how much of him is not done, how much is not piled?
The two Aśvins are the physicians of the gods.
Whatever of him is not done here, whatever is not piled, that he arranges with these, that he heals.
He lays down five of these.
Fivefold is Agni;
as great as Agni is, for him he makes a medicine.
With these he arranges the quarters of Agni.
With 'I set you on the back of Aditi,' [he lays them down];
with this [formula] he supports these worlds.
He lays down seasonal [bricks],
for the maintenance of the seasons.
He lays down five;
five indeed are the seasons;
as many as the seasons are, them he arranges.
They have identical beginnings, identical endings;
for the seasons have identical beginnings, identical endings.
He distinguishes them with a single call;
therefore the seasons are distinct;
therefore there are various forms of the same year.
He lays down the seasonal [bricks] after the bricks to the Aśvins;
he lays down the seasons upon the placed seed itself;
therefore the seed having been placed is born in accordance with the seasons.
He lays down the bricks to Vāyu after the seasonal [bricks];
he places the breath in the seasons;
therefore these, going around equally, do not decay;
therefore the wind repeatedly blows according to the seasons.
He lays down the water-bricks after the bricks to Vāyu;
therefore the wind carries rain;
with these he secures rain.
If he were to lay them down in one place, it would rain in one season;
he sets them around in turn;
therefore it rains season by season.
He lays down the age-bricks after the water-bricks;
the age-bricks are cattle;
in that he lays down the age-bricks after the water-bricks,
therefore cattle, having various minds and various habits, are of one mind regarding water.
Having laid down the water-bricks, he should lay down the age-bricks for one whom he would wish: 'May he have cattle.'
Water is indeed this agreement of cattle;
he piles the fire on the very agreement of cattle;
he becomes possessed of cattle.
Having laid down the age-bricks, he should lay down the water-bricks for one whom he would wish: 'May he be without cattle.'
He piles the fire on the disagreement of cattle;
he becomes without cattle.
He lays down four in front;
therefore in front the animal is narrower;
therefore there are four forms of the eye: two white and two black.
[They are] possessed of a summit;
therefore in front is the summit of animals.
[He lays down] five each on both sides,
for the coherence of the animal;
therefore the animal is inclined forward.
With 'The he-goat is the age,' he lays [one] down on the south;
with 'The ram is the age,' on the north;
he lays them down as its shoulders.
With 'The tiger is the age,' on the right wing;
with 'The lion is the age,' on the left [wing];
he places vigor in the two wings.
With 'The tiger is the age,' the eastern one;
with 'The lion is the age,' the western one;
therefore in front is born the tiger, behind the lion.
With 'Man is the age,' in the middle;
therefore man is the lord of animals.

31.13.m:
With 'Indra and Agni, [make] unswerving,' [he lays it down];
Indra and Agni are force and vigor.
This layer is the intermediate space;
the intermediate space is loose, as it were;
creatures are the intermediate space;
he places force and vigor in creatures.
It is naturally perforated,
for the emission of vital breaths, for the beholding of the heavenly world.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati;
in that he causes the horse to smell it,
he piles it with Prajāpati himself.
Now these are the quarter-bricks.
When the gods were going to the heavenly world, their quarters collapsed;
they saw these quarter-bricks;
with them they made firm the quarters.
In that these are laid down,
[it is] for the maintenance of the quarters.
With 'You are the queen, the eastern quarter,' [he lays it down];
therefore this is the queen of quarters.
With 'You are the virāj, the southern quarter,' [he lays it down];
therefore this rules over the quarters.
With 'You are the samrāj, the western quarter,' [he lays it down];
therefore here is imperial rule.
With 'You are the svarāj, the northern quarter,' [he lays it down];
therefore here is self-rule.
With 'You are the overlord, the high quarter,' [he lays it down];
therefore this is the overlord of the quarters.
He becomes all of these, who knowing thus lays these down.
With 'Protect my life,' he lays down ten in front;
nine indeed are the vital breaths in man, the navel is the tenth;
he places vital breaths in him.
With 'Grant me light,' [he lays down] the last one;
he places light as the highest;
therefore speech is the highest light among the vital breaths.
He lays down ten of these;
of ten syllables is the virāj;
the virāj is the light among meters;
the eye is light;
he places the eye in front of creatures.
With 'The mā is the meter,' on the south;
therefore the months turn to the right.
With 'The earth is the meter,' in the west,
for firm standing.
With 'Agni is the deity,' in the north;
Agni is force;
he places force from the north;
therefore one who advances from the north conquers.
He lays down twelve on the south,
twelve in the west,
twelve on the north;
they amount to thirty-six;
of thirty-six syllables is the bṛhatī;
the bṛhatī attained self-rule among meters;
he goes to self-rule who, knowing thus, lays these down.
Thirty-six are these;
of thirty-six syllables is the bṛhatī;
cattle are connected with the bṛhatī;
this layer is the intermediate space;
cattle are the intermediate space;
he places cattle in cattle.
Some vital breaths have their domain in Āditya, others have their domain in the Aṅgirases;
those in front have their domain in Āditya, those behind have their domain in the Aṅgirases.
With 'You are the summit, the ruler,' he lays down in front;
those vital breaths that have their domain in Āditya, those he thereby supports.
With 'The controller, the ruler,' in the west;
those vital breaths that have their domain in the Aṅgirases, those he thereby supports.
He lays down seven in front, seven in the west;
therefore the lower breath shares half of the upper breaths.
Prajāpati, having created creatures, desired regarding them: 'May I be the summit.'
He saw these head-bricks;
with them he sprang forth;
he became their summit.
That is the summit-nature of the head-bricks.
He becomes the summit of his equals who, knowing thus, lays these down.

31.16.m:
… ['You are the share of the barley,' on the south.]
The barley grains are indeed months;
the months are food;
he secures food on the south.
With 'You are the share of the Ṛbhus,' [he lays a brick down] in the west.
Thirty-three are the deities;
he becomes established in the deities themselves.
With 'The turning, the forty-eightfold [stoma],' [he lays a brick down] in the north.
Therefore the two hands are of equal strength.
This [fire altar] is arranged after the manner of the Arka;
food is the Arka; the Arka is Agni;
he eats food for whom, knowing thus, it is arranged.
Those which belong to the front he lays down in front;
a chief among his people is born to him who knows thus.
Those which are possessed of food [he lays down] on the south;
an eater of food is born to him who knows thus.
Those which are possessed of stability [he lays down] in the west;
he attains stability who knows thus.
Those which are possessed of force [he lays down] in the north;
a strong son is born to him who knows thus.
He who knows thus the arrangement of these—[everything] is arranged for him.

31.18.m:
With 'I set you in the seat of Ayu,'
he places life-span as the highest.
Therefore life-span is the highest of breaths.
[The brick] is naturally perforated,
for the emission of the breaths, for the revelation of the heavenly world.
The horse belongs to Prajāpati.
When he causes the horse to smell [the layer],
he piles it with Prajāpati himself.
Then this Vikarṇī [brick];
the Vikarṇī is indeed this stride of the gods;
he makes him stride along after the stride of the gods.
It is accompanied by Vāyu [the wind-mantra];
Vāyu is indeed breath;
the naturally perforated is life-span;
he places life-span and breath concordant in him.
When he piles [the fire altar], he kindles it.
He kindles it with the [brick] accompanied by Vāyu.
He should pile the fire altar for a full year.
The year is indeed Agni Vaiśvānara;
its bricks are the days and nights;
he piles it having obtained all the bricks;
he prospers.
If he should not pile it thus, he would deprive it of its dear body.
When he piles it thus, he enriches it with its dear body;
thus Vaiśvānara is piled with his body.
He lays down the seasonal [bricks],
for the upholding of the seasons.
He lays down two by two,
for the seasons are two by two.
He lays down four in the middle layer;
this middle layer is, as it were, the intermediate space;
the intermediate space is, as it were, loose;
four are the quarters;
he becomes established in the quarters themselves.
The gods measured out these [bricks] from the seasons;
that is the seasonal nature of the seasonal [bricks].
Whoever, knowing thus, lays these down becomes established in season after season as it arrives.
They have different beginnings and a common end;
therefore the forms of the same year are varied.
Having laid down an avakā-plant, he lays down a brick.
The avakā is indeed the womb of Agni Vaiśvānara;
he lays them down in their own womb,
for appeasement, not to burn.
He prospers who, knowing thus, lays these down.
Then these Viśvajyotis [bricks];
these are the breath-bearers of the sacrificer;
with the first he supports the out-breath, with the second the diffused breath, with the third the in-breath.
These deities lead to heaven;
through them he goes to the heavenly world.
These deities are luminous;
Agni becomes light for him in this world, Vāyu in the intermediate space, Sūrya in heaven;
all these worlds become luminous for him.
They are possessed of a ruler;
through them he attains rulership.
'Fill the space, fill the gap, and then sit firm;
Indra and Agni, Bṛhaspati [have seated you]';
Indra and Agni are force and vigor;
with force and vigor he piles it.
With 'They, the milkers of the well...';
therefore in knot after knot there is fluid.
With 'The spotted ones mix Soma...';
food is the spotted one;
he secures food.
With 'In the three luminous realms of heaven...';
three are the soma pressings;
he piles it in the pressings themselves.
An anuṣṭubh verse serves as attendant;
anuṣṭubh is breath;
therefore breath moves along through all the limbs;
anuṣṭubh is speech;
whatever of it here is not done, whatever is not piled, that he arranges through speech, that he heals.

31.19.m:
With those three gāyatrī verses dedicated to Agni that are possessed of a summit, he should lay down three [bricks] in the eastern half;
then with triṣṭubh verses, then with jagatī verses, then with anuṣṭubh verses, then with paṅkti verses, then with whatever meter belongs to Agni.
Three with one meter, three with one meter;
he should lay them down all along the fire [altar].
Yajñasena Caitra indeed knew this piling;
by it he indeed prospered.
He prospers who, knowing thus, lays these down.
The meters are cattle, the lovely thing of the gods;
he strips away the cattle, the lovely thing of his rival.
The Āhavanīya is the self; the dhiṣṇya is the rival.
If he were to lay them down in the Gārhapatya, he would place the lovely thing, cattle, in the rival;
he lays them down in the Āhavanīya;
he places the lovely thing, cattle, in his own self.
This same layer is called the high altar;
therefore these are laid down in the uppermost layer.
He becomes the highest among his equals who, knowing thus, lays these down.
Agni is an animal;
Prajāpati created the animals with Agni at their mouth.
In that there are five layers,
therefore cattle are said to be fivefold.
In that there are three layers,
threefold indeed is Agni.
In that there are two,
two-footed is the sacrificer,
for establishment.
Of two syllables is hair, of two syllables is skin, of two syllables is flesh, of two syllables is bone, of two syllables is marrow;
that is ten;
of ten syllables is the virāj;
therefore cattle are said to be virāj-like.
That which is knee-deep is the gāyatri-pile; that which is navel-deep is the triṣṭubh-pile; that which is the height of a man is the jagatī-pile.
One desiring heaven should pile [the altar] in the shape of a falcon, winged;
the falcon is indeed the swiftest of winged creatures;
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
One desiring establishment should pile [the altar] in the shape of an alaja bird, winged;
four are the quarters;
he is established in the quarters themselves.
One who desires, 'May I be possessed of a head in yonder world,' should pile [the altar] in the shape of a heron, possessed of a head;
he becomes possessed of a head in yonder world.
One should pile [the altar] in the shape of a fore-part of a chariot pole if he thinks, 'A rival has previously surpassed me';
by the fore-part pile the gods thrust away the asuras who had previously surpassed them;
whichever rival has previously surpassed him, him he thrusts away by it.
One should pile [the altar] in the shape of a fore-part of a chariot pole on both sides if he thinks, 'A rival is behind me';
whichever rival is behind him, him he thrusts away by it; he thrusts away the superior rival, he thrusts back the inferior rival.
One performing sorcery should pile [the altar] in the shape of a chariot wheel;
with the chariot-wheel pile the gods hurled the thunderbolt at the asuras, [saying], 'For so many [are you]!';
he hurls the thunderbolt at his rival and lays him low.
One desiring cattle should pile it as gathered together;
loose soil is indeed cattle;
he secures cattle.
One desiring kinsmen should pile it as round; kinsmen indeed surround [one];
he piles it round with his kinsmen.
One desiring food should pile it in the shape of a trough;
with a vessel food is eaten;
with that mouth he eats food.
One who desires, 'May I prosper in the world of the fathers,' should pile [the altar] in the shape of a burial mound;
by the burial-mound pile Yama prospered in yonder world;
he prospers in the world of the fathers.
One desiring heaven should pile [the altar] as a gāyatri-pile, winged;
Agni, having become a bird, flew to the heavenly world;
as they say: 'The gāyatrī, having become a bird, flew to the heavenly world,'
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
Some fire [altars] are piled facing upward, others facing downward;
those which are piled facing upward are for yonder world, those which are facing downward are for this world.
When standing upright he sprinkles with flakes of gold,
he thereby piles it facing upward,
for the attainment of the heavenly world.
